@meta
id: 13-10
title: One or more, and why the backslash
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-quantifiers
par: 17
minutes: 5
vscode: read the file top to bottom looking for a number
open: .env.example

@brief
An example env file ships with the keys and no secrets: values are
either blank or a harmless placeholder. Someone has committed a real
port number into this one. Find the key whose entire value is a number
and blank it — delete the value, keep the key and the equals sign.

You know the shape: an equals sign, then digits, then the end of the
line. `\d*$` looks right and is wrong — `*` allows *zero* digits, so
`=\d*$` matches every blank value in the file, and the first blank value
is on line one.

You want "one or more". Vim has it, and it comes with a backslash.

@keys
| `\+` | one or more of the atom before it |
| `*` | zero or more — no backslash |
| `\{n,m}` | between n and m — next lesson but one |
| `D` | delete from the cursor to the end of the line |

@hints
The blank values are the trap. Your pattern has to insist on at least
one digit between the `=` and the end of the line.
--
`\+` is "one or more". `=\d\+$` is an equals sign, at least one digit,
then the end of the line. Blank values do not qualify.
--
`/=\d\+$` `Enter` lands on the `=`. `l` onto the first digit, `D` to
delete the rest of the line.

@solution
/=\d\+$<Enter>  lD  :wq<Enter>

Fourteen keystrokes, and `VERSION=2.4.1` did not fool it either —
`\d\+$` has to reach the end of the line through digits alone, and the
dots stop it.

Now the backslash. In most tools, `+` means "one or more" and `\+` is
a literal plus. In vim's default dialect it is the other way round: `+`
is a plain character and `\+` is the quantifier. The same is true of
`?`, `{`, `(`, `)`, `|`, `<` and `>`. Only `.`, `*`, `[`, `^` and `$`
are special without help.

Vim calls this setting `magic`, and the reason is history: vi was built
for editing prose and code, where a literal `+` or `(` turns up far
more often than a quantifier, so those characters were kept literal by
default and the regex meaning put behind a backslash. `*` and `.`
were common enough as wildcards to earn the bare form.

The result is that vim's patterns, written plainly, look wrong to
anyone who has used regex anywhere else. `\d\+` instead of `\d+`,
`\(a\|b\)` instead of `(a|b)`. It is not wrong, it is a different
dialect, and there is a switch that turns the familiar one back on. It
gets a lesson of its own shortly. Until then, learn the escaped forms;
you will meet them in other people's vimrcs and in every vim answer on
the internet.

@start .env.example
NODE_ENV=
LOG_LEVEL=info
DB_HOST=database
DB_PORT=
DB_NAME=orders
DB_USER=
DB_PASSWORD=
RABBIT_URL=amqp://rabbit-1:5672/payments
VERSION=2.4.1
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=2525
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
STRIPE_SECRET=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
SENTRY_DSN=

@expected .env.example
NODE_ENV=
LOG_LEVEL=info
DB_HOST=database
DB_PORT=
DB_NAME=orders
DB_USER=
DB_PASSWORD=
RABBIT_URL=amqp://rabbit-1:5672/payments
VERSION=2.4.1
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
STRIPE_SECRET=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
SENTRY_DSN=
